WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.107
- [Marc] Castles are built to last, right?

00:00:11.107 --> 00:00:14.330
One hundred years, 500
years, a thousand years.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:18.060
So why build a castle that
only exists for three months

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.143
and has to be rebuilt every
single year when it melts?

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.343
Well, what if that was the point?

00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:28.440
What if you were building it
not for the finished product,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.889
but just 'cause you like building stuff?

00:00:29.889 --> 00:00:32.030
(chainsaw buzzing)

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:33.730
- We're just creating for fun,

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.750
creating something that's going to melt.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:37.150
- [Marc] And because you
want to make something

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.040
that looks like it
belongs in a fairy tale.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.060
- I'm Sleeping Beauty.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:41.220
It's nice to meet you.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.960
- I was just blown when I saw it.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:44.890
- Blown away.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.010
- [Marc] Even if it can't last forever.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.547
But then, the best
things in life never do.

00:00:49.547 --> 00:00:52.630
(excavator rumbling)

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.810
So in what faraway land of ice and snow

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.790
might you find this unique castle?

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.641
Tell me at what state to be?

00:01:04.641 --> 00:01:07.213
- America.
- New Hampshire.

00:01:07.213 --> 00:01:09.270
- [Marc] This is the
aptly-named White Mountains

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.630
National Forest.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.580
Now, there's no doubt that
this is one of the most

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.430
beautiful parts of the country.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:17.290
But people who don't like
the cold tend to stay away

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.900
because temperatures here have been known

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.830
to fall as low as minus
100 degrees Fahrenheit

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:22.740
with wind chill.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.643
Yes, minus 100 degrees.

00:01:27.047 --> 00:01:30.940
- We have trees, and
we have a lot of land,

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.471
and we have the Ice Castles.

00:01:33.471 --> 00:01:36.780
(ethereal music)

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.100
- [Marc] For the last four winters

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.300
this hidden corner of
New England has been home

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.880
to the Ice Castle, a gargantuan structure

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.850
made up of over 25 million pounds of ice.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.330
Thousands of visitors come
to explore the turrets,

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.390
tunnels, slides, and sculptures that seem

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.730
to have sprung out of
the ground like magic.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.858
- They have really fun
slides with lights in them.

00:01:56.858 --> 00:01:58.941
- I'm ready for anything!

00:02:00.865 --> 00:02:03.593
- And a fire-breathing princess.

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:08.367
(screaming, exclaiming)

00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:10.100
(whooshing)

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.301
- I was scared she was gonna
blow up the whole thing down.

00:02:12.301 --> 00:02:15.700
Then we call the fire department,
and then something bad

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.313
would happen, and maybe
she would get arrested.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.500
- Arabelle and Lily are best friends,

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.630
or worst enemies, depending on the day.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:28.320
Arabelle completely looks up to Liliana.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.893
I think she wants to be like Lily one day.

00:02:30.893 --> 00:02:32.029
- Come on!

00:02:32.029 --> 00:02:33.448
Whoa!

00:02:33.448 --> 00:02:35.474
Why do I keep on slipping?

00:02:35.474 --> 00:02:37.251
(shrieking)

00:02:37.251 --> 00:02:38.757
- [Marc] How did you feel when you first

00:02:38.757 --> 00:02:40.490
saw the Ice Castle?

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:42.300
- I felt amazing.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.410
- I felt like how did they build this?

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.233
It could be all magic.

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:54.150
- Um, they might have
done magic, I don't know.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.457
- [Marc] The Ice Castle is,
in fact, built with magic,

00:02:58.457 --> 00:03:00.930
but it's the kind of magic
that results from a group

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.713
of craftspeople working in
subfreezing temperatures

00:03:03.713 --> 00:03:05.970
for a cumulative 8,000 hours.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.440
(chainsaw buzzing)

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.550
- We actually harvest
our own icicles here,

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:12.210
and then we take them and stick them

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.400
where we want the ice to grow.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.240
And then we turn on the sprinkler system

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.540
and overnight it'll grow together,

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.940
and once it grows together
it's strong enough

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.650
to get up on top, and we
keep building from there.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:23.707
- [Marc] And there's
another group of people

00:03:23.707 --> 00:03:26.840
who help shape the castle
without even knowing it:

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.200
the visitors themselves.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.440
- I like for people to
touch it, and get in it,

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.250
and see them interacting with the ice.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:34.830
They're essentially helping me carve

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.500
because the heat from
people's mittens and bottoms

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.490
will continue to carve the
castle and smooth it out.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.360
I'm gonna try to knock off this part.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.470
- Is it fun?

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.950
- [Woman] Yeah, it is fun.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:45.987
- Is it like all year?
- It's really fun.

00:03:45.987 --> 00:03:50.987
No, no, we start building
in December, usually,

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:52.820
when it gets cold enough.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:56.160
I think you have to be 18,
but you just apply online.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.010
I really like telling
children how it's made.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:00.880
I'm like, this is the next
generation of artists.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.000
I like to work with happy accidents,

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.630
and ice is great because you don't

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:07.080
have a lot of control over it.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.470
It's all up to the weather,
and it changes all the time.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.840
New Hampshire, it's a tough place to live

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.420
because you have to be
able to handle the winters.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:16.960
I think everyone's a little stir-crazy,

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:18.100
and you just don't know what to do

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.650
when you're trapped in
your house all the time,

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.253
and especially if you have kids.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.990
I feel like art could
help to make connections

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:25.200
and work through things.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.966
- How do you spell sadness?

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:28.640
- S-A-D-N.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.810
- Art is a great way to
work through problems,

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:34.090
and emotions, and putting
emotions into something.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:34.923
- Is that you?

00:04:34.923 --> 00:04:35.756
- Mm hmm.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.900
- I feel for my children
because for most of their life

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.050
it was almost picture-perfect,
and then it went from

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.220
perfect to crumbling really fast.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.224
- Ow, wow.

00:04:48.224 --> 00:04:49.400
- [Woman] Whoa, okay.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.823
- Well, my mom and my
dad got in a divorce.

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:00.090
Sometimes stuff is just for the best,

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:05.090
and sometimes that's just gonna happen.

00:05:05.244 --> 00:05:07.744
(quiet music)

00:05:08.883 --> 00:05:12.370
- Christmas wasn't like
all the Christmases before.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:16.890
So we went to the Ice
Castles, and I think it was

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.780
just the pick-me-up we all kinda needed.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.093
It was a day away from
reality, and it was magical.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.910
Maybe it's not a bad thing
that they're experiencing

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:30.950
heartbreak so early on
because they're learning

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.370
that there is life after heartbreak.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.260
You can go through
something and you can be

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:37.430
in a lot of pain, and
you can get through it,

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.173
and you can come out stronger.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:44.530
- If Arabelle's sad, I
would give her a hug,

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:47.430
and say, just try to think
of the good times you had,

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:50.053
or the good times that you will have.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:55.920
- [Marc] All good things come to an end.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.290
Maybe that's what helps us realize

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:58.590
that they are good things.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.120
And besides, who knows?

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.360
Something even better
might be around the corner.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:04.820
- I'm happy to see it melt.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:06.080
It needs to be renewed.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:07.013
It needs to melt.

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:09.950
- [Marc] Sometimes,
though, mother nature needs

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:11.049
a little bit of help.

00:06:11.049 --> 00:06:14.407
(excavator beeping)

00:06:14.407 --> 00:06:16.010
(ice crumbling)

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.960
The Ice Castle is so big,
that even when temperatures

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.321
get above freezing it
has to be knocked down

00:06:21.321 --> 00:06:24.540
because it takes too long to melt.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:25.860
- I definitely embrace change.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.780
Dive into a season, and
then as soon as you're sick

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.200
of that season, that season's gone,

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:31.070
and there's a new season.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:33.350
When spring comes, I start to garden.

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:35.600
Soon as the drips of the
Ice Castles start happening,

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.730
I was like, OK, let's
have some springtime.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.590
I want to see plants
burst out of the ground,

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:40.853
and I want to tackle them.

00:06:40.853 --> 00:06:43.603
(cheerful music)

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.482
- [Marc] Are you excited
to go back next year?

00:06:46.482 --> 00:06:48.000
- [Together] Yes.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.550
- I'm so excited because
maybe there will be

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:56.550
another fire-breathing
princess, and the slides

00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:58.573
hopefully will still be there.

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:03.393
And maybe there'll be a little change.

00:07:03.393 --> 00:07:04.226
Mm hmm.

